          </div>
        </header>
        <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.1fr)_minmax(0,0.9fr)]">
          <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>Iowa officially launched its Purple Star School Program on April 23, 2026, marking a major milestone in how the state supports military-connected students and their families.</p>
            <p>This effort did not appear overnight. It was built through collaboration, persistence, and a shared belief that military kids deserve consistent support no matter where life takes them. Leaders across education and military communities worked together to bring the vision to life, including Chris Cox, a member of the Military Interstate Children's Compact Commission, who helped advocate for the legislation that made the program possible.</p>
            <p>In 2025, Senate File 275 was signed into law with unanimous bipartisan support, laying the foundation for Iowa's Purple Star designation. With implementation underway, Iowa became the 44th state to adopt the nationally recognized program.</p>
            <p>The official inauguration took place at the Iowa State Capitol during the Month of the Military Child. Seven school districts, representing 17 schools, were recognized as Iowa's first Purple Star Schools. Each demonstrated a strong commitment to supporting military-connected students through practical systems, visible resources, and ongoing staff readiness.</p>
            <p>At the ceremony, state and military leaders came together to recognize the impact of the initiative and the schools leading the way. The message was clear: when schools understand and support military families, students are better positioned to thrive.</p>
            <p>Military-connected students often face frequent moves, disrupted learning, and time away from a parent during deployment. The Purple Star designation helps ensure schools are not only aware of those challenges, but actively equipped to respond to them.</p>
            <p>As more schools pursue the designation, Iowa continues building a stronger, more connected support system for military families across the state.</p>
